## 2. INCIDENT POST-MORTEM: ROUTE INTERCEPTION SYNTAX CRASH
* **Incident Date:** June 2026
* **Severity:** High (Broken E2E Performance Assertions)
* **Status:** Resolved

### 2.1 Problem Description
During initial testing runs of the specialized high-concurrency automated load script (stress.test.js), the execution suite threw an unhandled fatal syntax error stating that the unexpected token "<" was not valid JSON.

### 2.2 Root Cause Analysis (RCA)
Review of the Express application layer revealed a route precedence conflict. The application’s static file middleware (app.use(express.static)) and catch-all roots were registered higher up in the execution file than the new diagnostic telemetry pathways. 

When the automated load testing script fired requests at /debug/perf, Express intercepted the stream early and returned the client-side user interface homepage (index.html starting with HTML doctype syntax) instead of letting the request hit the JSON telemetry data block.

### 2.3 Corrective Action / Resolution
The core application layout inside app.js was refactored by hoisting all specialized telemetry middleware and the /debug/perf endpoint to the absolute top of the route instantiation stack. Insulating these diagnostic channels from static asset handlers completely resolved the content negotiation conflict, enabling clean JSON stream parsing under load.

